20 Developer And Maintainer Guide
*********************************

This guide is for people changing Emacsvox source, documentation,
integrations, speech-server protocols, build machinery, or release
staging.  It is included at the end of the published manual so that an
installed Info manual and an offline checkout remain complete, but it is
not part of the ordinary installation and use path.

   Before making a governed change, read ‘AGENTS.md’ and every accepted
record in ‘docs/adr/’.  Use the Emacs selected by ‘local.mk’ for
repository checks.  The generated *note Emacsvox Reference Manual:
(emacsvox-reference)Top. is the exact inventory of Lisp commands,
options, and keymaps; the chapters that follow explain contracts and
procedures that cannot be generated from those interfaces.

* Menu:

* Documentation Maintenance::    Author, validate, and publish Emacsvox documentation.
* Speech Server Protocols::      Understand speech-server protocols.
* Integration Maintenance::      Maintain optional integrations and release staging.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Documentation Maintenance,  Next: Speech Server Protocols,  Up: Developer Guide

20.1 Documentation Maintenance
==============================

This chapter is for contributors changing the maintained manuals,
generated reference, or public browser documentation.  Reports and
internal notes do not require an Emacsvox build; use the smallest
documentation target that covers the source being changed.

20.1.1 Choose the canonical source
----------------------------------

The maintained Org master is ‘docs/manual/emacsvox.org’.  Current-user
chapters live under ‘docs/manual/chapters/’, while developer and
maintainer chapters live under ‘docs/developer/’.  The source layout and
fast authoring loop are summarized in ‘docs/manual/README.org’.

   The current project overview lives in ‘README.org’, and the complete
user installation path lives in ‘docs/manual/chapters/installation.org’.
Source checkout, byte-code, and release procedures live in
‘docs/developer/integration-maintenance.org’.  Submit changes through
the Emacsvox repository at <https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox>.
Before changing a governed workflow or architecture, read ‘AGENTS.md’
and every accepted record under ‘docs/adr/’.  Repository-local
‘local.mk’ selects the Emacs used by supported targets; Emacsvox
requires Emacs 30.2 or newer.

   Do not hand-edit generated references.  ‘info/docs.texi’ and
‘info/keys.texi’ come from the source documentation generator, and
‘etc/aural-presentation-reference.org’ comes from
‘utils/emacsvox-aural-audit.el’.  The first two sources form the
separately installed ‘emacsvox-reference.info’ manual.  Selected
historical chapters form the separately installed
‘emacsvox-heritage.info’ manual.

20.1.2 Use the fast authoring workflow
--------------------------------------

Keep current operating instructions separate from historical accounts.
State which backend or platform a procedure applies to, avoid
duplicating detailed protocol contracts across repositories, and link to
the owning document.  Apply the user-versus-developer audience test in
ADR 0004 before choosing a source directory.

   While editing Org prose, preview the complete manual without
inspecting or rebuilding Lisp byte-code:

     make docs-org-preview

   Use ordinary Org code markup for keys (‘C-x C-f’ or ‘M-x term <RET>’)
and verbatim markup for other literals (‘local.mk’ or ‘make
docs-update’).  The Texinfo exporter preserves keyboard and named-key
formatting; standard Org text and HTML exports retain the key text.  Use
Org heading links within the manual and described ‘info:’ links to other
manuals.

   Run ‘make docs-org-text’ for a complete UTF-8 text copy at
‘.docs-preview/org-manual/emacsvox.txt’.  Set ‘DOCS_ORG_TEXT’ to choose
another output path.  This also uses the standard Org exporter without
loading Emacsvox.

   The ignored Info, Texinfo, and HTML previews appear under
‘.docs-preview/org-manual/’.  Once the text settles, run ‘make
docs-update’ to update the tracked generated Texinfo body and checked
Info files.  Do not edit those generated files directly.

   When public Lisp documentation or keys change, first bring byte-code
current with ‘make bytecode’.  Use ‘make bytecode-rebuild’ instead after
changing macros or public interfaces, as described in ‘AGENTS.md’.  Then
run ‘make docs-reference’.  ‘make docs-generate’ remains the
comprehensive reference and manual update.

   Before review, merge, or deployment, run the non-mutating ‘make
docs-release-check’; ‘make docs-check’ is its compatibility alias.
Commit the regenerated Info artifacts with their source changes.

20.1.3 Publish the browser manual
---------------------------------

Org on ‘master’ is the canonical maintained manual source.  GitHub Pages
serves the generated static files committed on the separate ‘gh-pages’
branch; never edit those HTML files as source.  Publishing is
deliberately explicit:

  1. Finish the manual change, update its checked Info output, run the
     release validation target above, and commit the complete
     source-side change on ‘master’.

  2. Check out that exact commit in a clean source worktree.  Build its
     local, ignored byte-code with the Emacs selected by ‘local.mk’ or
     an ‘EMACS=’ override.  Use a separate sibling worktree checked out
     on ‘gh-pages’ as the publication directory.

  3. From the clean source worktree, run:

          make docs-publish-pages DOCS_PUBLISH_DIR=/path/to/emacsvox-pages

     The target validates the manual and publishes that same staged
     render, replaces only its managed HTML files, writes ‘.nojekyll’,
     and records the source commit, Emacs version, and Texinfo version
     in ‘emacsvox-source.txt’.  It refuses an uncommitted source tree.

  4. Inspect the ‘gh-pages’ worktree, including the provenance file and
     the main browser entry points.  Commit the generated site with the
     source commit in the commit message, then push ‘master’ and
     ‘gh-pages’.  The GitHub Pages source setting must name the root of
     ‘gh-pages’.

   No documentation target creates a commit or pushes a branch.  This
keeps the published bytes reviewable and makes publication a distinct
maintainer action.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Server Protocols,  Next: Integration Maintenance,  Prev: Documentation Maintenance,  Up: Developer Guide

20.2 Speech Server Protocols
============================

Emacsvox produces spoken output by communicating with one of several
speech servers.  Two protocol families coexist:

   • The legacy command-and-queue line protocol is implemented by
     standalone Tcl servers and retained by Omnivox for compatibility.

   • Capability-negotiated Omnivox control, marker, logical-voice, and
     structured presentation protocols carry bounded JSON records beside
     that legacy stream.

   This chapter is primarily for speech-server and client developers.
The detailed Omnivox contracts are owned by the Omnivox repository; this
manual summarizes their relationship to Emacsvox and then documents the
legacy line protocol.

   For additional notes on how to log and view TTS server commands when
developing a speech server, see the historical Emacspeak article at
<http://emacspeak.blogspot.com/2015/04/howto-log-speech-server-output-to-aid.html>.

20.2.1 Omnivox Structured Protocol
----------------------------------

At process startup the Emacsvox adapter requests capabilities and live
engine inventory.  Features are used only after negotiation; an older
server continues on the legacy path.  Current Emacsvox requires
‘presentation_timeline_v3’ before it submits an Aural Presentation as a
structured timeline.

   Concrete Aural plans retain a bounded compatibility lowering when
those features are absent.  Speech, voice commands, silence, tones,
dispatch, and interruption use the established legacy queue commands.  A
legacy tone cannot preserve the explicit overlay clock: both insert and
overlay modes become an ordered ‘t’ command, and a fractional frequency
is rounded to the nearest hertz.  The surrounding speech continues
rather than being rejected for that loss of presentation precision.

   Guarantee-bearing operations do not invent success on a legacy
server.  Playback completion, source markers, and domain-scoped
replacement remain unavailable when the selected server cannot
acknowledge them truthfully.

   Ordinary control requests belong to their speech process.  They have
a 30-second response deadline and an admission limit of 64 outstanding
requests per process.  Background inventory refreshes share one pending
request per process.  Capability negotiation uses
‘omnivox-control-negotiation-timeout’.  Previews, parameter queries,
palette application, and synchronous library operations retain their own
deadlines and cancellation rules.

   The client detaches a request and cancels its timer before invoking
its callback.  A response deadline or process exit produces a local
error response with the original request ID, ‘:origin client’, and code
‘timeout’ or ‘process_closed’.  This does not prove that a submitted
server operation was cancelled or had no effect.  Late replies cannot
change the completed outcome.  Ordinary navigation Stop leaves metadata
requests pending; each speech lane owns its requests independently.

   When adding an ordinary control caller, use
‘omnivox--send-control-request’.  Its callback may run during the send.
Use the internal ‘managed’ timeout only when the enclosing operation
already enforces a deadline and calls ‘omnivox--take-control-request’ on
every cancellation or nonlocal exit.  Private preview operations keep
their existing playback ownership and reserved callbacks.  No control
wire version changes for this client lifecycle handling.

   A complete timeline contains its delivery policy, replacement domain,
generation, dispatch ID, speech spans, and positioned actions.  Small
timelines use one bounded frame; large timelines use a bounded multipart
envelope that is validated and admitted atomically.  Decodable rejected
or stale direct timelines receive terminal status so Emacs callbacks can
retire their dispatch records.

   ‘ordered’ and ‘urgent’ timelines are admitted immediately.  Only
matching ‘replaceable’ work uses the short server-side coalescing
window.  Receipt of a newer valid replaceable generation immediately
cancels queued, synthesizing, and buffered work in the same replacement
domain.  It does not globally stop ordered, urgent, legacy, or
differently keyed output.

   Marker events report playback start and positioned playback
boundaries; tracked status reports terminal completion.  With opt-in
Aural diagnostics, Emacs records successful dispatch send, first
received ‘utterance_started’, and terminal observation under the same
dispatch ID.  Omnivox's normal session log records protocol admission,
worker queue wait, individual engine attempts, audio queueing, first
mixer-source consumption, and terminal playback under that ID.  This
correlation uses existing marker and terminal records rather than adding
a wire-protocol version.  Playback markers track mixer-source
consumption and may precede physical sound by the output device's buffer
latency; a protocol write or terminal callback is therefore not proof of
acoustic onset.

   Recent Feedback retains a separate, data-only playback snapshot.
Owned, validated start and voice-choice events associate actual physical
voices with the frozen registry layers and retained run ranges.  A
presentation shares a 64 KiB evidence budget, with at most 128
dispatches and 128 spans per dispatch; speech text remains subject to
the existing 4096-byte history limit.  Exhausted or incomplete evidence
disables original replay without blocking delivery.  Completed snapshots
drive exact private voice previews with fallback disabled.  They never
rewrite the named-voice registry or borrow another lane's latest
logical-voice realization.  Ordinary history navigation retains the
requested annotations; explicit original playback uses this observed
snapshot.

   The canonical protocol documents are:

   • Omnivox Control Protocol
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/protocols/control.md)

   • Omnivox Presentation Timeline Protocol
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/protocols/presentation-timeline.md)

   • Omnivox Helper Protocol
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/protocols/helper.md)

20.2.2 Legacy Line Protocol
---------------------------

The TTS server reads commands from standard input.  Script
*speech-server* can be used to cause a TTS server to communicate via a
TCP socket.  Speech server commands are used by the client application
to make specific requests of the server; the server listens for these
requests in a non-blocking read-eval-print (REPL) loop and executes
requests as they become available.  Requests can be classified as
follows:

   • Commands that send text to be spoken.

   • Commands that set *state* of the TTS server.

   All commands are of the form

     commandWord {arguments}

   The braces are optional if the command argument contains no white
space.  The speech server maintains a *current state* that determines
various characteristics of spoken output such as speech rate,
punctuations mode etc.  (see set of commands that manipulate speech
state for complete list).  The client application *queues* The text and
non-speech audio output to be produced before asking the server to
*dispatch* the set of queued requests, i.e.  start producing output.

   Once the server has been asked to produce output, it removes items
from the front of the queue, sends the requisite commands to the
underlying TTS engine, and waits for the engine to acknowledge that the
request has been completely processed.  This is a non-blocking
operation, i.e., if the client application generates additional
requests, these are processed *immediately*.

   This design allows a legacy Emacsvox TTS server to be *highly*
responsive; Client applications can queue large amounts of text
(typically queued a clause at a time to achieve the best prosody), ask
the TTS server to start speaking, and interrupt the spoken output at any
time.

  1. Testing A Direct Legacy Server

     This protocol-level test is only for a retained direct
     Tcl-compatible server.  Run its launcher from the Emacsvox
     repository root, for example:

          ./servers/espeak

     Then enter:

          q {This is a test.}
          d
          s

     The ‘q’ command queues text, ‘d’ dispatches it, and ‘s’ stops
     speech.  Type ‘C-d’ to close the server.  A backend-specific README
     may provide a stronger diagnostic and should take precedence over
     this generic protocol test.

  2. Commands That Queue Output.

     This section documents commands that either produce spoken output,
     or queue output to be produced on demand.  Commands that place the
     request on the queue are clearly marked.

          version

     Speaks the *version* of the TTS engine.  Produces output
     immediately.

          tts_say text

     Speaks the specified *text* immediately.  The text is not
     pre-processed in any way, contrast this with the primary way of
     speaking text which is to queue text before asking the server to
     process the queue.

     Note that this command needs to handle the special syntax for
     morpheme boundaries ‘[*]’.  The ‘[*]’ syntax is specific to the
     Dectalk family of synthesizers; servers for other TTS engines need
     to map this pattern to the engine-specific code for each engine.
     As an example, see ‘servers/outloud’ A morpheme boundary results in
     synthesizing compound words such as *left bracket* with the right
     intonation; using a space would result in that phrase being
     synthesized as two separate words.

          l c

     Speak *c* a single character, as a letter.  The character is spoken
     immediately.  This command uses the TTS engine's capability to
     speak a single character with the ability to flush speech
     *immediately*.  Client applications wishing to produce
     character-at-a-time output, e.g., when providing character echo
     during keyboard input should use this command.

     Omnivox clients that negotiate ‘palette_letter_v1’ and its complete
     layered voice bundle use ‘emacsvox_letter {BASE64_UTF8_JSON}’
     instead.  Its version 4 or 5 timeline envelope has one layered span
     containing one visible Unicode scalar, ordered delivery, no
     replacement key, and no actions.  Registration and validation
     precede interruption.  The command retains isolated-character rate,
     uppercase pitch, pronunciation and short streaming startup while
     applying the palette's ‘default’ voice, actual fallback tuning and
     native parameters.  Playback receipts use the existing layered
     timeline contracts.  Legacy servers continue to receive ‘l’;
     ordinary phonetic words and punctuation names use the normal
     palette speech path.

          d

     This command is used to *dispatch* all queued requests.  It was
     renamed to a single character command (like many of the commonly
     used TTS server commands) to work more effectively over slow (9600)
     dialup lines.  The effect of calling this command is for the TTS
     server to start processing items that have been queued via earlier
     requests.

          s

     Stop speech *immediately*.  Spoken output is interrupted, and all
     pending requests are flushed from the queue.

          q text

     Queues text to be spoken.  No spoken output is produced until a
     *dispatch* request is received via execution of command *d*.

          c codes

     Queues synthesis codes to be sent to the TTS engine.  Codes are
     sent to the engine with no further transformation or processing.
     The codes are inserted into the output queue and will be dispatched
     to the TTS engine at the appropriate point in the output stream.

          a filename

     Cues the audio file identified by filename for playing, an ‘ogg’
     file.

          p filename

     *dispatch* play the audio file identified by filename for playing —
     ‘ogg’ file.

          t freq length

     Queues a tone to be played at the specified frequency and having
     the specified length.  Frequency is specified in hertz and length
     is specified in milliseconds.

          sh duration

     Queues the specified duration of silence.  Silence is specified in
     milliseconds.

  3. Commands That Set State

          tts_reset

     *Immediately* reset TTS engine to default settings.  Stops all
     speech and clears the queue.

          tts_set_punctuations mode

     Queues setting TTS engine to the specified punctuation mode.
     Typically, TTS servers provide at least three modes:

        • None: Do not speak punctuation characters.

        • Some: Speak some punctuation characters.  Used for English
          prose.

        • All: Speak out *all* punctuation characters; useful in
          programming modes.

          tts_set_speech_rate rate

     *Immediately* change speech rate.  The interpretation of this value
     is typically engine specific.

          tts_set_character_scale factor

     Queues changing the scale factor.  Scale factor applied to speech
     rate when speaking individual characters.Thus, setting speech rate
     to 500 and character scale to 1.2 will cause command *l* to use a
     speech rate of *500 * 1.2 = 600*.

          tts_split_caps flag

     Queues changing of the state of *split caps* processing.  Turn this
     on to speak mixed-case (AKA Camel Case) identifiers.

          tts_sync_state punct splitcaps caps rate

     *Immediately* apply the passed settings.

     This ensures *atomicity* i.e., all state settings in the TTS engine
     happen at one shot.  Note that failure to do this might result in
     some utterances being spoken with a partially set state.

        • punct: see tts_set_punctuations

        • splitcaps: see tts_split_caps

        • caps: engine specific implementation of capital letter
          handling

        • rate: see tts_set_speech_rate

          set_next_lang say_it

     *Immediately* switch to the next language on the server, maintained
     internally on the TTS server.  If say_it is non-nil say the
     language change via the TTS.

          set_previous_lang say_it

     *Immediately* switch to the previous language on the server,
     maintained internally on the TTS server.  If say_it is non-nil say
     the language change via the TTS.

          set_lang language:voice say_it

     *Immediately* switch to the requested language and/or voice,
     separated by a colon.  Examples

        • set_lang "en"

        • set_lang "en:whisper"

        • set_lang ":whisper"

     If say_it is non-nil, speak via the TTS the language and voice
     selected.

          set_preferred_lang alias lang

     *Immediately* set an alias in the TTS server mapping for example
     "en" to "en_GB". This can later be used by set_lang.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Integration Maintenance,  Prev: Speech Server Protocols,  Up: Developer Guide

20.3 Integration Maintenance
============================

This chapter collects source-level contracts that are deliberately
omitted from the user task guides.  Optional package APIs and renderers
evolve, so recheck the upstream interfaces and keep compatibility
behavior bounded when changing an integration.

* Menu:

* Authoring Sound Packs::        Prepare a personal resource directory and validate its cue metadata.

20.3.1 Logical table readers
----------------------------

‘emacsvox-table-reader.el’ supplies common cell/row/column formatting,
copying, and movement decisions.  Agent Shell retains its rendered
source-property adapter, contextual map, public commands, and
preferences.  Markdown and Org register source adapters and use an
explicit reader minor mode.  The table browser uses a buffer-local
adapter for shared inspection, copying and speech preferences.  Its own
movement commands keep the standalone view at vertical boundaries.
Tabulated lists register an explicit reader adapter while retaining
their ordinary application navigation outside that context.

   An adapter supplies a fresh cell snapshot and a native Aural
submission function.  Snapshots contain complete logical rows, flat
source positions in row order, table bounds, optional header
information, and zero-based internal coordinates.  Movement rejects
missing cells and handles boundaries before changing point.  Source
adapters rebuild snapshots for each command; source edits end explicit
reading.  No native table editing command is used for navigation.
Presentation remains under each integration's Aural module and role,
with one main-lane submission per reader action.

   The tabulated-list adapter reads printed entry properties in display
order, without calling entry producers.  It uses full string/button
labels and column metadata, skips invisible rows, and maps an empty
final cell to its newline.  Refreshes retire the reader before positions
can become stale.  An optional adapter map preserves application and
button actions; only shared navigation and ‘q’ deliberately override
them during reading.  Other occupied keys retain their application
action, with shared commands accessible through ‘M-x’.  Standalone list
bounds include the buffer, so vertical edges stay put.

   The read-only table browser caches logical string rows when
rendering.  Text properties map every character, including embedded line
breaks and delimiters, to a model cell.  Pre/post-command hooks
synchronize the model after ordinary character movement; snapshot
extraction also synchronizes it.  Browser movement uses the model and
its rendered position index.  New reader actions submit under module
‘table-ui’, with role ‘field’ for cells and explicit main-lane delivery.
The local browser map is independent of the legacy speech-prefix map.
CSV parsing preserves quoted fields and visited source buffers before
constructing the view.  Sorting constructs a new view and carries over
both filters and the shared speech preferences.

   The shared prefix adds only previously unused keys: ‘C-r’ toggles
source-table reading, and ‘[’ / ‘]’ discover tables.  Printable reader
keys are contextual.  Control-Meta arrows also enter reading and move
within supported source tables.  A conditional minor-mode map uses
menu-item filters: returning nil outside these tables preserves the
effective binding in lower maps, including personal bindings.  The
filter preserves point and match data, excludes code examples, and
defers to the active reader map after entry.  First-arrow entry and
movement produce one submission.  Structural movement remains available
outside tables; inside tables, its usual ‘C-M-f/b/u/d’ keys remain
available.  Org's native ‘<TAB>’, ‘<RET>’ and Meta-arrow editing
bindings remain available outside reading.  Agent Shell's existing
scoped overrides and prompt behaviour remain as documented in its
integration contract.

   Regression coverage lives in ‘test/emacsvox-table-reader-tests.el’
and the Agent Shell table cases.  The graphical Agent Shell integration
gate includes real wrapped-table movement and copying.  The
source-reader graphical case
‘emacsvox-table-reader-graphical-wrapped-source’ must also run in a
fresh graphical Emacs with Markdown Mode installed; its batch skip is
not a pass.  Check source text and modified state, effective keys,
complete wrapped values, source edits, code exclusion, title
preferences, and native Aural submissions.  Browser coverage lives in
‘test/emacsvox-table-ui-tests.el’; use
‘EMACSVOX_TABLE_UI_TEST_LOAD=compiled’ in a fresh Emacs after building
to verify the compiled implementation.  Its
‘emacsvox-table-ui-graphical-multiline-navigation’ case requires a
graphical display and checks actual wrapping and logical cell movement.
Listening acceptance is separate from submission and graphical checks.
Tabulated-list coverage lives in
‘test/emacsvox-tabulated-list-tests.el’, including full values, blanks,
buttons, native action keys, sorting, filtering, refresh and hidden
rows.  Its graphical navigation case must run on a real graphical Emacs;
a batch skip does not establish display acceptance.

20.3.2 Ordinary line movement in short windows
----------------------------------------------

Core ‘next-line’ advice dynamically disables ‘auto-window-vscroll’ for
an interactive one-line forward move when the displayed row fits in the
selected window.  Native partial scrolling can otherwise scroll without
moving point, causing the existing after advice to read the same line
again.  This reproduced while reading Notmuch HTML with a header line
and mixed font heights.

   Preserve the interactive marker for the after advice, programmatic
movement, prefix arguments and scrolling within genuinely tall rows.  Do
not change the buffer's scrolling preference or suppress repeated text
as a substitute for correct movement.  The graphical integration gate
exercises real rendering, cursor movement and speech, plus a tall-image
case, in ‘test/emacsvox-vertical-navigation-tests.el’.

20.3.3 EAT rendered output and prompt observation
-------------------------------------------------

EAT output is collected around ‘eat-term-process-output’ before
redisplay can trim it, including a bounded unfinished row.  Never retain
raw process bytes or unredacted concealed text.  Read fresh public
terminal bounds, preserve soft-wrap continuity, and reject uncertain
clear/reset/alternate-screen replacements.  Check selected-window
following after EAT synchronizes windows, not between chunks inside its
renderer.  Editable line-mode text lies after ‘eat-term-end’; it can
share the final physical row with the terminal cursor.

   Prompt observation wraps the public ‘ui-command-function’ terminal
parameter and preserves its original callback.  EAT has already parsed
the protocol: ‘e;B’ and ‘e;C’ bracket a prompt, and ‘e;J’ identifies a
new shell prompt cycle.  The adapter reads bounded rendered ranges,
excludes those ranges from ordinary output, and deduplicates
same-position prompt repaint.  It does not infer a prompt from
punctuation or require annotation overlays.  Keep this protocol seam
covered against the pinned EAT source and its real Bash integration when
upgrading.  Unmarked unfinished text remains ordinary output; otherwise
remote questions can disappear indefinitely while waiting for a newline.

   Foreground loss discards Emacs-local automatic work only.  It must
not call a process-wide speech stop.  Already dispatched speech may
finish; scoped Omnivox cancellation remains a separate backlog
direction.  The graphical gate covers real X11 focus loss, minibuffers,
selection, line drafts and scrolling.  Integration runners force the
silent ‘log-null’ backend before loading Emacsvox so a failed test
cannot start the maintainer's configured speech engine.  Submission
checks are separate from the maintainer's final audible SSH acceptance.

   When aural diagnostics are enabled, ‘eat-output-decision’ records
explain collection rejection, pending-work cancellation, retained
output, and selection for presentation.  Each contains fixed
decision/reason symbols plus terminal, generation and update
identifiers, without terminal text.  Selection for presentation is not
proof of transport, playback or audible output; correlate the ordinary
aural submission and Omnivox records for those later stages.

20.3.4 Source checkout and byte-code lifecycle
----------------------------------------------

Use the Emacs selected by the ignored repository-local ‘local.mk’.  When
Emacs 30.2 or newer is not the system ‘emacs’, set its absolute path
there:

     EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs

   Run ‘make check-emacs’ before diagnosing compilation.  After an
ordinary Lisp edit, use the incremental build:

     make bytecode

   After switching branches, pulling changes, or changing a macro or
public interface, use:

     make bytecode-rebuild

   That target removes ignored generated ‘.elc’ files and recompiles
them with the selected Emacs; it does not clean or discard source
changes.  ‘make bytecode-check’ is the non-mutating preflight for
startup or deployment.  It reports missing, stale, orphaned, or
wrong-Emacs byte-code.

   Each ‘make config’ fully regenerates ‘lisp/emacsvox-loaddefs.el’ from
the current sources, including edited autoloads, new modules, and
deleted modules.  The build targets ‘make bytecode’, ‘make
bytecode-rebuild’, ‘make emacsvox’, and ‘make all’ run configuration
before compiling; ‘make compat-test’ runs it before testing.  The
portable command ‘emacs -Q --batch -l etc/configure-emacsvox.el’ uses
the same full regeneration.  Saving a source file or starting Emacs does
not regenerate autoloads.  Neither ‘make bytecode-check’ nor ‘make
docs-release-check’ does so.  Regenerating the index alone does not
force all modules to be recompiled.

   Tests deliberately prefer source and therefore do not prove that the
byte-code used by a live profile is current.  Verify rebuilt byte-code
in a fresh Emacs rather than relying on a file reload when compiled
dependants may already be resident.

   During development, load the changing setup source explicitly:

     (load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")

   The setup entry point warns and prefers source when newer Lisp would
otherwise be hidden by stale local byte-code.  In a stable checkout with
current ‘.elc’ files, ‘(load-library "emacsvox-setup")’ may be used.  Do
not use ‘load-library’ as the first load after editing the source tree:
Emacs can select an old ‘emacsvox-setup.elc’ before the newer source
checker runs.

  1. Background startup ownership and failure recovery

     ‘emacsvox’ starts two named background tasks: ‘core-advice’ loads
     core command feedback and ‘package-preparation’ registers optional
     package adapters.  The private task registry retains each thread,
     state, start/end times, and original error for the Emacs session.
     Repeated startup reuses these records, including completed and
     failed tasks, instead of starting duplicate background work.  Other
     startup effects, including speech-server initialization, still run
     when ‘emacsvox’ is called again; this is not a general-purpose
     retry command.

     Registration completion is separate from adapter loading.  An
     already-loaded package triggers its adapter immediately; another
     package gets a deferred load callback.  Each package/adapter pair
     is registered once.  Both load paths record failures without
     aborting later registrations.  Failed or completed adapters are not
     automatically retried on another package load.  User quit is
     recorded and still propagates.  Source fallback from the setup
     guard is preserved in both background tasks and deferred callbacks.

     ‘M-x emacsvox-startup-status’ speaks a summary and selects a Help
     report with task durations and original adapter errors.  Calling
     ‘emacsvox-startup-status’ from Lisp returns the summary without
     speech or a window.  Failures also issue a warning and attempt a
     notification in interactive Emacs; retained records remain
     available if either feedback path fails.  Successful completion
     adds no new unconditional announcement.  The protocol and routing
     checkpoints defined in the startup ADRs retain their separate
     meanings; task completion does not declare the speech server ready,
     or establish that every adapter has loaded.

     Correct the reported cause and verify in a fresh session,
     rebuilding changed Lisp first.  A failed adapter can leave partial
     advice or hooks: error isolation does not roll those effects back.
     Do not clear private ownership tables as a retry mechanism, since
     deferred callbacks can still refer to their records.  Retain error
     details, selected Emacs version, and phase durations when
     debugging.  Background scheduling is preserved; these records do
     not guarantee that core advice finishes before the first input
     command, and batch timings do not measure interactive startup
     latency.

     The focused tests exercise real deferred loads, failure isolation,
     task reuse, source fallback, and spoken status with speech
     intercepted.  Check source with the selected Emacs using
     ‘test/emacsvox-startup-tests.el’.  For fresh compiled parity, first
     run ‘make bytecode-check’, then set
     ‘EMACSVOX_STARTUP_TEST_LOAD=compiled’ when loading the same test
     file in a new batch Emacs.  This selects ‘emacsvox.elc’ explicitly;
     it does not make every test dependency compiled.  Audible diagnosis
     and real startup latency still need an isolated interactive
     session.

  2. Supported Emacs versions

     The Lisp runtime and all platform routes support Emacs 30.2 and
     newer.  Run ‘make compat-test’ for the core compatibility
     contracts.  Settings menus require Transient 0.13.3 or newer; Emacs
     30.2 needs a separate package.  CI uses the pinned Transient,
     cond-let, and compat sources for that build and Emacs 31.1's
     bundled Transient for the newer build.  Both compile all maintained
     modules.  The full ‘make test’ suite additionally requires the
     optional integration packages, including ‘lua-mode’ on Emacs 30.

     The separate core speech CI job runs ‘make core-test’, ‘make
     trace-test’, and ‘make compiled-aural-test’ on both versions with a
     private home directory.  It covers speech, voice configuration,
     transport, Voice Workbench, startup, and golden speech traces
     without optional Emacs packages.  It requires GNU Make, Git, a
     POSIX shell, and Tcl (‘tclsh’); missing prerequisites fail before
     the core tests run.  The fresh CI checkout exercises source,
     followed by an isolated compiled Aural check.  Optional integration
     suites remain outside this job.

     The separate integration CI job runs Agent Shell, Notmuch, and EAT
     tests on both versions with a private home directory and eight
     reviewed dependency snapshots, including Transient 0.13.3 and
     cond-let 1.0.0.  ‘make test-deps’ verifies the archive hashes in
     ‘test/integration-dependencies.json’ and stages sources and
     autoloads under ignored ‘.test-deps/’; it never changes personal
     packages.  ‘make integration-test’ verifies that prepared tree
     before loading it and runs the batch cases.  ‘make
     graphical-integration-test’ uses the same verified tree for the two
     Agent Shell display-row cases in a real graphical Emacs on a
     private Xvfb display.  Both gates fail on missing dependencies or
     skipped selected tests.  The graphical runner requires ‘xvfb-run’,
     ‘Xvfb’, ‘xauth’ and ‘timeout’, captures its log, disables speech
     output and stops its isolated session after 60 seconds.  CI runs
     both gates on each supported Emacs version.  Pin updates require
     upstream review, new archive hashes, and fresh preparation for both
     Emacs versions.  These source tests capture speech events and
     exercise disposable EAT subprocesses; they do not replace listening
     tests or compiled integration checks.  See ‘test/README.org’ for
     preparation checks, directory overrides, recovery, and coverage
     boundaries.

     Keep each Emacs version's build in a separate checkout; never reuse
     ‘.elc’ files across versions.  All platforms, including native
     Windows x64, use this minimum.  The documentation toolchain retains
     its independent pin.

  3. Native Windows builds

     Use a separate checkout on a Windows drive.  The native installer
     regenerates autoloads and all modules from the ‘lisp/Makefile’
     inventory with the selected Windows Emacs.  Its launcher verifies
     that byte-code in a fresh process.  Do not share generated
     byte-code with a WSL checkout using another Emacs.

     Run the native installer checks from that Windows checkout:

          .\test\emacsvox-windows-install-tests.ps1 -Emacs "C:\path\to\emacs.exe"
          .\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -Role Desktop -Check
          .\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -Role Desktop
          .\bin\emacsvox.ps1 -Check

     The PowerShell tests use temporary fixtures and an existing native
     Emacs.  They check doctor behavior, explicit selections,
     prebuilt/offline preflight, incomplete installations, platform
     rejection, checksums, archive paths, argument handling, and atomic
     configuration replacement.
     ‘test/emacsvox-native-bytecode-tests.el’ compares the native
     inventory with GNU Make and checks dependency order and byte-code
     preflight failures.  Keep the WSL x64/ARM64 and remote installer
     tests passing.

     Actual acquisition acceptance must start with the pinned archives
     in an empty installation root, not a reused developer runtime.  For
     ‘-DownloadEmacs’, verify the complete upstream Windows ZIP and its
     bundled runtime features.  For source builds, start with a private
     toolchain.  Keep MSYS2 package versions,
     compiler/configure/build/install logs, and ‘emacsvox-build.json’
     with the installed Emacs.  The installation logs also include
     ‘native-install-provenance.json’ with Lisp source hashes and
     selected pins.  MSYS2 packages are signed rolling inputs; these
     records do not claim a frozen or bit-identical compiler
     environment.  Verify fresh graphical startup, acknowledged routing,
     and tracked completion on both speech lanes with a PATH that
     contains neither WSL nor MSYS2 tools.  Human listening remains a
     separate acceptance step.  Native Windows x64 is supported; remote
     workstation speech remains preview, as recorded in ADR 0014
     (../adr/0014-native-windows-support.org).

       1. Offline Windows development bundle

          ‘make windows-bundle-dev’ downloads checksum-pinned Emacs and
          Omnivox archives and creates a development ZIP below ‘dist/’.
          Packaging needs Python 3.11 or newer and Git on the build
          machine.  To use a prepared cache without network:

               python3 utils/emacsvox-windows-bundle.py --offline \
                 --cache-directory /path/to/archives --output-directory dist/windows-installer
               make windows-bundle-test

          Extract the ZIP on a local Windows drive and run
          ‘Install.cmd’, or run ‘Install.ps1 -InstallRoot "C:\path with
          spaces\Emacsvox"’ from PowerShell.  The destination needs
          neither Git nor build tools.  ‘-Check’ verifies the bundle
          without writing installation state; ‘-NoAudioCheck’ prepares
          an unattended installation.  Run the printed launch command
          with ‘-Check’ to perform the graphical speech check afterward.

          From a Windows checkout, repeat acquisition and installation
          acceptance with:

               .\test\emacsvox-windows-bundle-tests.ps1 -BundleArchive "C:\path\to\bundle.zip" -AudioCheck

          This tests a fresh install, a repeated install, damaged
          inputs, long paths, profile preservation and protection of
          edited sources.  It limits PATH to Windows tools.  Add
          ‘-KeepInstallation’ to retain the successful test directory;
          failed test directories are retained with their logs
          automatically.

          Each bundle records its source commit and hashes the exact
          payload, including uncommitted changes.  It copies only source
          files, compiles in a versioned Windows application directory,
          and refuses modified installed sources.  Repeated installation
          reuses the verified runtimes and preserves the profile.  The
          ZIP is a local development artifact.  To add the Windows setup
          wizard, stage its contents for Inno Setup:

               make windows-setup-stage WINDOWS_BUNDLE=/path/to/development-bundle.zip \
                 WINDOWS_SETUP_STAGE=/path/to/new/setup-stage
               make windows-setup-test

          Put the complete staging directory on a Windows drive.  From a
          native Windows checkout, build the executable with:

               .\utils\emacsvox-windows-setup-build.ps1 -StagingDirectory "C:\path\to\setup-stage" -InstallCompiler

          ‘-InstallCompiler’ acquires the checksum-pinned Inno Setup
          compiler and verifies its publisher signature before
          installing it per-user on the build machine.
          ‘etc/windows-setup.conf’ owns these build-tool pins.  An
          existing pinned compiler may be selected with ‘-Compiler’.
          The output directory contains the setup executable, checksum
          and compiler/payload provenance.  No compiler is needed on the
          destination machine.

          Run setup-helper checks and real lifecycle acceptance on
          Windows:

               .\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-helper-tests.ps1
               .\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-cleanup-tests.ps1
               .\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-tests.ps1 -Setup "C:\path\to\first-setup.exe" -Upgrade "C:\path\to\next-setup.exe" -AudioCheck
               .\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-wizard-tests.ps1 -Setup "C:\path\to\setup.exe"

          The lifecycle test refuses to replace an existing development
          Setup registration or matching shortcuts.  It tests an
          isolated installation, shortcut launch, running-process
          refusal, repair, an optional second-build upgrade, and
          uninstall while retaining profile and voice fixtures.  It
          keeps logs for diagnosis.  Use a separate test account when a
          development Setup installation is already registered.  Test
          interactive wizard navigation with screen readers separately;
          silent acceptance does not establish that UI.  The wizard test
          inspects native accessibility controls, reaches the review
          page and cancels without installing.  It does not replace
          listening with a screen reader or keyboard-only navigation.

          For rollback acceptance, stage an intentionally failing
          fixture with ‘python3 test/prepare_windows_setup_failure.py
          --bundle /path/to/bundle.zip --staging-directory
          /path/to/failure-stage’ and compile it using the same build
          command.  Pass its executable as ‘-FailedUpgrade’ to the
          lifecycle test.  This fixture fails during configuration,
          before activation; the test checks that the previous selection
          and launcher still work.  Keep the failure fixture separate
          from user-facing artifacts.

          The main GitHub Actions CI workflow builds the installer on
          Windows x64 for pushes and pull requests to ‘master’, and can
          also be started manually.  Its Linux packaging fixtures run
          first.  The Windows job checks native ownership and cleanup
          helpers, verifies the cached Emacs and Omnivox downloads, and
          uses the pinned Inno compiler.  It tests installation,
          shortcut launch, running-process refusal, repair, rollback of
          a deliberately failed upgrade, and uninstall with profile and
          voice fixtures preserved.  Destination checks run with only
          Windows tools on PATH.  The core speech CI job also exercises
          startup and welcome-screen preferences on each supported Emacs
          version without needing an audio device.

          The ‘emacsvox-windows-installer-evidence’ artifact retains
          build manifests, checksums, compiler provenance and lifecycle
          logs for 14 days, including failed runs.  To export lifecycle
          logs locally, add ‘-LogDirectory’ with a new directory to
          ‘emacsvox-windows-setup-tests.ps1’.  Its ‘result.json’ records
          whether acceptance passed and which optional checks were
          requested.

          CI does not run audio or interactive screen-reader acceptance.
          It does not yet exercise a successful second-build upgrade;
          use ‘-Upgrade’ locally for that check.

          Successful runs offer two separate downloads in the job
          summary: ‘emacsvox-windows-installer’ contains the tested
          setup executable, its checksums, provenance, launch
          instructions and ‘SOURCE-DOWNLOADS.md’, linking directly to
          matching third-party sources on their upstream hosts.
          ‘emacsvox-windows-sources’ contains our exact Emacsvox code,
          build scripts, runtime assets and the same source index.
          Unshipped historical soundscapes and talk recordings are
          omitted.  It does not mirror the large upstream archives.
          Sources are optional and are not installed on users' machines.
          CI uploads them first and retains them for 90 days, compared
          with 14 days for the installer.  Keep paired sources available
          if redistributing an installer, and remove binaries before
          their sources when deleting artifacts.  Keep the upstream
          locations accessible; repair broken source links or withdraw
          the affected installer.

          ‘etc/windows-sources.json’ binds the reviewed Emacs/Omnivox
          runtime archives to source archives and licence notices.  It
          records exact DLL package matches, additional MSYS2 sources
          absent from the GNU dependency ZIP, and locked Rust
          dependencies.  ‘etc/windows-sources.txt’ explains the archive
          contents and build recipes.  The application includes
          ‘etc/WINDOWS-THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt’; Emacs and Omnivox also
          retain their own notices.  Runtime updates require a source
          and notice review; link the exact versions with their
          checksums.

          The packaging gate requires a clean committed checkout
          matching the staged installer, intact payloads and compiler
          provenance.  It verifies the pinned upstream archives in a
          private build cache and publishes their links beside the
          installer.  It checks both downloads before making either
          available.  Only the main tested executable is uploaded.  The
          failed-upgrade fixture and offline bundle remain build inputs.
          Run ‘make windows-sources-test’ for the isolated
          source/publication boundary checks.

          The setup executable remains a development artifact.  Stable
          release integration and remaining acceptance work are recorded
          in ADR 0024 (../adr/0024-native-windows-installer.org).
          Public preview promotion is described below and in ADR 0025
          (../adr/0025-windows-preview-publication.org).

  4. Public Windows installer previews

     Use a Windows preview when testers need public downloads without
     Actions sign-in or artifact expiry.  Keep ‘VERSION’ and the
     ‘Unreleased’ NEWS heading unchanged.  Do not use the stable release
     targets or a stable version tag for this operation.  The tag
     identifies the installer source commit; the clean publication
     tooling checkout may be newer and records its own commit.

     Start with a successful complete ‘CI’ run from ‘master’ in the
     repository selected by ‘RELEASE_REMOTE’ (default ‘origin’).  The
     installer, matching sources and evidence artifacts must still be
     available for preparation.  For example:

          make windows-preview-test
          make windows-preview-prepare WINDOWS_PREVIEW_RUN=RUN_ID \
            WINDOWS_PREVIEW_TAG=windows-preview-2026-09-20 \
            WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory
          make windows-preview-check WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory

     Preparation downloads and verifies the exact CI assets.  It checks
     all archived Emacsvox source files against Git, the installer and
     setup provenance, source pairing, upstream source index, and native
     lifecycle result.  It writes the publishable files under ‘assets/’
     and a local ‘preview.json’ receipt.  It creates no tag or GitHub
     release and refuses to overwrite an existing output directory.
     Review ‘assets/README-preview.md’, the executable, sources and
     checksums.  These instructions include permanent source links; the
     old CI-only README is not published.  Upstream sources remain
     upstream links, not mirrored archives.

     Only after explicit maintainer approval naming the preview, run:

          make windows-preview-tag WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory
          make windows-preview-publish WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory

     Both commands repeat validation.  The first creates only a local
     annotated, unsigned tag at the tested source commit.  The second
     pushes that exact tag, creates a private GitHub draft, attaches
     sources and installer, verifies all remote SHA256 values, then
     publishes as a prerelease with Latest disabled.  It always selects
     the repository explicitly from ‘RELEASE_REMOTE’'s push URL.  It
     does not edit the installer or source ZIP.  If uploading fails,
     rerun the publish target against the same prepared directory; it
     resumes only a matching draft with intact assets.  A mismatched
     draft, conflicting tag or published release is an error, never an
     invitation to force-push or overwrite it.

     Changing the publication tooling commit or prepared files requires
     preparing a new directory.  Keep the original evidence for review.
     The preview is for native Windows x64, remains unsigned, and does
     not establish stable installer readiness.  Its notes identify the
     remaining interactive and lifecycle checks.

  5. Remote preview validation

     Keep remote installer fixtures separate from connected acceptance.
     In the Omnivox checkout, run ‘make dev’ followed by ‘make
     remote-test’; set ‘OMNIVOX_REMOTE_TEST_SLOW=1’ to cover heartbeat
     expiry.  Set ‘OMNIVOX_REMOTE_TEST_EMACS’ to the selected Emacs and
     ‘OMNIVOX_REMOTE_TEST_EMACSVOX’ to the matching Emacsvox checkout
     for client acceptance.  The Omnivox ‘docs/REMOTE.md’ guide records
     Windows relay tests, real-device options, and retained evidence.
     Null-output tests do not establish audible playback or acceptance
     through a separate SSH host.  Remote access remains preview
     independently of native Windows desktop support.

20.3.5 Debian package builds
----------------------------

‘make deb’ builds a local development package in ‘dist/’ using Python 3,
‘dpkg-deb’, Git and the selected Emacs.  It stages runtime sources and
generates autoloads in a private directory, preserving checkout
byte-code and personal configuration.  The deb has architecture ‘all’,
an Emacs 30.2 dependency (including terminal and graphical
alternatives), and an Omnivox recommendation.  It loads source and does
not install emacsen-common hooks or compile on install.  The ownership
and release boundaries are recorded in ADR 0013
(../adr/0013-debian-package.org).

   ‘make deb-test’ checks the actual archive, reproducibility, source
startup, remote diagnosis, dependency metadata and refusal of an
unprepared release.  CI additionally installs, reinstalls and purges the
package in a disposable Ubuntu 26.10 container using
‘test/debian-install-smoke.sh’.  This target supplies Transient 0.13.3
or newer through apt; Ubuntu 26.04's package is too old.  The check
loads settings through the isolated launcher and reports the actual
Transient version and path.  CI uploads the development deb and checksum
as artifacts, without publishing a release.

   After preparing the checked source archive using the release
procedure below, ‘make release-deb’ produces
‘dist/emacsvox_VERSION-1_all.deb’ and its checksum.  The script itself
verifies the existing clean-source artifact gate before and after
packaging.  Local versions instead contain ‘+git’ with the source commit
and source hash, and revision ‘-0local1’.  The included
‘BUILD-INFO.json’ records the exact source identity and autoload Emacs
version.  Neither package target creates a tag, uploads release assets,
adds an apt repository or changes the manual release publication
approval boundary.

   After a maintainer publishes a stable GitHub release, the Release
Debian package workflow checks out its exact annotated tag and downloads
the source archive, checksum and provenance from that release.  It
checks the source identity, installs from the archive, builds the
release deb, and tests install, startup, reinstall and removal in Ubuntu
26.10 before attaching the deb and checksum.  Build jobs have read-only
repository access; only the attachment job can write release assets.  A
rerun accepts identical existing assets and refuses to replace different
files.  CI does not create tags or releases.

   This workflow uses Emacs 31.1 for source-archive installation and
autoload generation, with its supported bundled Transient.  The Ubuntu
installation check exercises the packaged Emacs 30.2 and external
Transient route.  The recorded build toolchain can differ from a locally
prepared package.  Let CI attach the release deb instead of uploading a
differently built local package with the same name.

20.3.6 Emacsvox release staging and publication
-----------------------------------------------

The repository-level ‘VERSION’ file is the canonical stable release
identifier.  Calendar-versioning policy, including delayed patches and
backports, is recorded in ADR 0002
(../adr/0002-calendar-versioning.org).  When advancing a release, update
‘VERSION’, the literal package header in ‘lisp/emacsvox.el’, and the
current heading in ‘etc/NEWS’ together, then run:

     make version-check

   Between releases, ‘etc/NEWS’ uses the ‘Emacsvox Unreleased’ heading,
so changes can be recorded before deciding whether the next publication
is a patch or a new feature-release series.  After publishing, preserve
those notes verbatim as ‘etc/NEWS-VERSION’ and start a new unreleased
file.  Ordinary ‘version-check’ accepts that state; ‘release-check’,
‘release-tag’, and ‘release-publish’ require the current NEWS heading to
match ‘VERSION’ exactly.

   Prepare an artifact only from a separate clean, committed worktree.
Build fresh byte-code and run the guarded artifact target:

     make bytecode-rebuild
     make release-artifact

   ‘release-artifact’ runs the complete test and documentation gates
before creating ‘dist/emacsvox-YYYY.MONTH.PATCH.tar.bz2’, its SHA-256
checksum, and a provenance record naming the checked source commit.  For
a patch-zero feature release, the series must match the current month.
A corrective or backport release retains its original non-future series
as required by ADR 0002.  The source archive includes the maintained
‘utils/’ tools needed by the installers, launchers and build.  Before
recording checked provenance, the artifact target extracts the actual
archive into a temporary directory and runs the remote-only installer
with the selected Emacs in a private home.  It checks fresh compiled
startup, the offline manual and the remote helper's credential
validation without opening a workstation connection or playing speech.
‘make source-archive-check’ repeats this check on an existing archive;
‘make source-archive-test’ checks export contents and rejection of
missing helpers, local configuration and byte-code.  CI runs both checks
before a release as well.  On native Windows, run
‘test/emacsvox-source-archive-tests.ps1 -Archive PATH -Emacs PATH’ to
check the actual archive's installer doctor, complete native compilation
and fresh byte-code validation in a temporary Windows directory.

   Inspect the archive, checksum, and provenance before proceeding.

   Stop after artifact inspection unless fresh explicit permission names
the release to be tagged and published.  Preparing an artifact,
committing or pushing a branch, publishing documentation, or receiving a
general instruction to continue is not permission to create or push a
release tag.  Once that separate authorization has been given, the
guarded commands are:

     make release-tag
     make release-publish

   ‘release-tag’ validates the artifact against the still-clean ‘HEAD’
and creates a local annotated, unsigned tag; it does not push the tag.
The unsigned-tag policy and its trust boundary are recorded in ADR 0006
(../adr/0006-annotated-unsigned-release-tags.org).  ‘release-publish’
repeats the artifact and source-commit validation, pushes only that
exact tag, and creates the GitHub release in the repository identified
by ‘RELEASE_REMOTE’.  This explicit selection prevents the GitHub CLI
from choosing an ‘upstream’ remote when releasing from a fork.  No
release target pushes ‘master’ or the documentation branch.  Use ‘make
release-check’ alone when a non-mutating preflight is wanted without
creating an artifact.

20.3.7 Future Aural contract decisions
--------------------------------------

The completed Aural implementation diary is preserved under
‘archive/emacsvox/migrations/’ rather than serving as current
instructions.  Three questions from that diary remain deliberately
undecided and require an explicit architecture decision before
implementation:

   • whether a critical alert may be completely suppressed or must
     retain an alternative presentation;
   • which deterministic ordering policy would apply before minor-mode
     selectors can participate in the presentation cascade; and
   • whether a new presentation occasion belongs to the core semantic
     contract or to a module-owned extension.

   Do not infer answers from the archived proposals.  Current behavior
is defined by the Aural implementation, its generated reference, and
accepted ADRs.

20.3.8 Voice-style metadata ownership
-------------------------------------

The private ‘emacsvox-aural--voice-style-fields’ table in
‘lisp/emacsvox-aural-rules.el’ owns accepted field names, groups, value
types, and integer bounds.  The existing public dimension lists and
private accepted key list are derived from it; validation traverses the
descriptors directly.  Composition and provenance continue to consume
the accepted-key inventory.

   Keep the two established orders explicit.  Validation checks preset,
family, the four numeric ACSS fields, post-synthesis fields, relative
rate, and legacy rate.  The accepted-key inventory places relative and
legacy rate before the post-synthesis fields.  ACSS dimension order also
determines positional legacy personality settings, and rich dimension
order determines adjustment rows.

   Every accepted field allows ‘nil’.  Validation preserves the original
plist, including duplicate fields, for which the first value is used.
Field absence, explicit ‘nil’, and zero remain distinct during
composition and persistence.  Legacy ‘:rate’ is accepted for loading
saved data but is not applied as a new rate; nonzero legacy values
produce the existing retuning degradation.  The table supplies no
implicit defaults and does not declare engine support.

   An explicit ‘nil’ over a preset is a compatibility behavior to
characterize, not an authoring decision settled by the metadata table.
For example, the current compiler clears the reported average-pitch
value for ‘(:preset base :average-pitch nil)’ while retaining the base
voice command and emitting no pitch-reset command.  The tests preserve
this distinction.  A future inheritance, omission, or reset policy needs
a separate decision.

   Compiler conversion, backend wire names, capability discovery,
normalization, and delivery stay in their existing owners.  When adding
a field, update those consumers explicitly and test final serialized
output or an explicit degradation.  The common voice editor uses the
shared field metadata for bounds and neutral values.  It covers portable
ACSS and optional rate and post-synthesis adjustments in the same
retained draft.

   Input grammar remains UI policy.  In the common voice editor, blank
numeric entry requests the adapter default; zero is explicit.
Individual choice fields distinguish shared inheritance, custom values
and explicit adapter defaults.  Rule-editor input has its own scoped
inheritance semantics; do not substitute one editor's blank-input
behavior for the other's.

   The common editor displays low-pass and high-pass as amounts from
zero through nine, increasing with stronger filtering.  Low-pass
converts between amount and stored cutoff with ‘9 - value’; high-pass
retains its numeric direction.  Zero is labelled neutral.  Stored
cutoffs and adapter-default nil retain their meaning.  Invalid boundary
steps must fail before history, dirty state, refresh or audition
changes.

   Delivery preparation has the limited helper boundaries described
below; asynchronous operation ownership remains a separate refactoring
proposal.

   The rule tests state field inventories, bounds, error precedence, and
composition expectations independently of the metadata.  Scheme tests
cover saved rich palettes and explicit-nil rules; transport tests check
distinct ACSS mappings, zero and omitted wire fields, effects, and
preset behavior.  ‘make compiled-aural-test’ also runs these style
contracts with the isolated compiled subsystem loaded, checking that the
implementations stay in that build.  The ‘voice-style-ui’ tests exercise
common-editor entry, inherited reset and draft retention in source and
compiled runs.  Editor tests also cover numeric bounds, explicit
defaults, undo and comparison.  They capture auditions and do not start
audio.  Run ‘make graphical-voice-test’ for the voice editor's link,
return navigation, and save-button checks in a fresh graphical Emacs
with current byte-code.  It requires Xvfb, xvfb-run and xauth, and uses
a separate virtual display so test windows cannot take desktop focus.
The launcher prints the graphical ERT log and exits unsuccessfully for
failures, skips, startup errors or a 45-second timeout.  A missing
virtual-display tool fails before starting Emacs.  The graphical
redisplay test is reported as skipped in ordinary batch runs.  The
connected saved-style regression edits a custom ‘bolden’, reads its
saved data back, and compares common-editor and Workbench previews with
the final normal-speech wire for ‘voice-bolden’.  Workbench uses the
compiler's palette resolution and the shared effect normalizer: gain and
pan level five must remain exactly neutral at 0.5.  Preserve both this
path and the independently specified Workbench scale cases when changing
preview or palette lookup.  Preserve final-write, timeout, reentrancy,
and trace regressions when working on later delivery slices; metadata
validation alone cannot establish process lifecycle or audible behavior.

20.3.9 Timeline preparation boundaries
--------------------------------------

Two private helpers in ‘lisp/emacsvox-aural-transport.el’ make
preparation inputs explicit.
‘emacsvox-aural--timeline-effect-transition’ takes previous effects,
normalized next effects, and a span ID, returning a directive and the
next state.  Replacement copies the saved state separately from the
directive; retention preserves the previous state.  The builder still
owns the state accumulator, span numbering, and when normalization runs.
Suppressed or empty content does not advance effects, while spoken
boundary actions do.

   ‘emacsvox-aural--encode-timeline-delivery-fields’ takes a policy and
replacement key.  The existing
‘emacsvox-aural--timeline-delivery-fields’ wrapper reads the dynamic
submission variables at the end of timeline construction.  Preserve that
capture point: resource resolution earlier in construction can re-enter
Lisp and change the submission state.  The serializer preserves ordered
defaults, key conversion, circular printing, diagnostics, and byte
bounds.

   The complete builder is not pure.  Cue resolution through
‘omnivox-remote-resource’ may perform I/O; relative-rate support comes
from the live speech process.  Those operations, action placement,
dispatch registration, framing, fallback, and process writes retain
their existing owners and sequence.  Separating a complete delivery
candidate from those effects requires further design and failure-path
characterization.

   The ‘delivery-preparation’ tests in
‘test/emacsvox-aural-transport-tests.el’ check complete serialized
timelines and final writes, semantic bindings, state ownership, policy
inputs, and capture timing.  The compiled Aural gate also runs these
contracts and existing stop and send-failure regressions with the
compiled implementation resident.  Keep the full failure, reentrancy,
and golden-trace suites as regression checks; captured writes establish
protocol behavior, not audible playback.

20.3.10 Aural interface source ownership
----------------------------------------

Ordinary-buffer entry captures a source buffer, a marker, and a guarded
source line.  Child interfaces and Help inherit that context through the
inspection and shared UI helpers.  Use
‘emacsvox-aural-inspection-call-in-source’ for contextual commands so
that moving the source cursor does not retarget an open interface.
Re-entry from an ordinary buffer deliberately captures a new target.  A
dead or replaced source is reported rather than resolved through an
unrelated recent-buffer fallback.

   Current-item lookup may reuse a frozen history record only when the
captured source identity, position, and modification tick still match.
Source identity is a buffer-local, data-only token that distinguishes
buffers sharing a name; history continues to retain no buffer objects or
markers.  Explicit historical selection remains valid after the source
changes and is explained from its frozen plan.  Prepared current-item
rules carry a source guard checked before their saved or live rule layer
changes.

   Find rule editors by scope and target identity, not by a formatted
buffer name.  Buffer scopes belong to buffer objects, fragment scopes to
fragment identifiers, and personal/session scopes each share one working
editor.  Reopening a dirty editor preserves its draft and source
association.

20.3.11 Remote speech recovery
------------------------------

Ordinary ‘tts-initialize’ startup schedules the existing remote retry
when main speech starts but a configured notification connection fails.
The scheduler respects ‘omnivox-remote-auto-reconnect’, explicit
suspension, and its single pending timer.  The retry restores only
missing lanes; it keeps a working main process and negotiates fresh
configuration for the new notifier.  ‘omnivox-remote-disconnect’ cancels
the timer and suspends even a retry callback that has already been
dispatched.  Local speech and disabled notification streams do not start
remote recovery.  Exercise these boundaries in
‘test/omnivox-remote-tests.el’ with controlled connection failures and
timers.

20.3.12 Omnivox voice configuration applies
-------------------------------------------

‘omnivox-apply-voice-configuration’ captures one generation and its
definitions for the original speech processes.  Applies may overlap.
Each owns its request identifiers and retires only those callbacks when
a process result becomes terminal.  Acknowledging routing precedes
logical registration; one aggregate reports applied, partial, or failed
after all participating processes finish.

   The timeout closes pending results as failed with phase ‘timeout’,
cancels the timer, and releases the apply's pending callbacks.  A late
acknowledgement cannot start another phase, update acknowledged
configuration, or publish a second terminal result.  A reply from a
stopped or replaced process also cannot advance its old apply.  Request
ownership must remain correct when replies are dispatched while a send
is still returning.

   This closes client work; it cannot undo a change already sent to the
server.  The Workbench therefore describes a timeout as an unconfirmed
server outcome and offers reapplication of the committed profile.  No
late-reply history is retained for expired applies.  Keep request-table,
late-reply, overlapping-apply, and replacement regressions in
‘test/emacsvox-tts-tests.el’ and spoken timeout feedback coverage in
‘test/emacsvox-aural-voice-workbench-tests.el’.

20.3.13 Notmuch contracts
-------------------------

The live module is registered as an optional package integration.  Its
stable extension points are the Hello, Search, Show, and Tree mode
hooks; Search and Show mode maps; public interactive commands; and
public Search/Show accessors.  Advice is installed only when a target
exists and is target-aware, so a nested or programmatic upstream call
does not claim the outer user's feedback.

   The Show landing and visibility paths also read renderer metadata
such as ‘:headers-overlay’, ‘:message-visible’, message extents,
‘:notmuch-part’, part-hidden state, and Notmuch part-button types.
Missing optional metadata should reduce presentation detail rather than
authorize an operation.  Review these adapters whenever the Notmuch
renderer changes.

   Each user-owned asynchronous Search process carries its own
completion state as a process property.  The upstream sentinel renders
first; Emacsvox then clears that property before delivering exactly one
terminal result.  The owning buffer and process status are
authoritative.  A killed owner remains silent, concurrent buffers cannot
overwrite one another, and the global refresh-all path intentionally
acquires no ownership.

   Run ‘make notmuch-test’ for the explicit source suite.  After current
byte-code exists, run ‘make compiled-notmuch-test’; it verifies the
exact loaded ‘emacsvox-notmuch.elc’, compiler version, and the same
focused selector, including real displayed-window reading for both
advance paths.

20.3.14 Agent Shell contracts
-----------------------------

Use ‘M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable’ to reinstall speech hooks after
deliberately disabling them or while testing live integration changes.

   The minimum supported Agent Shell is 0.85.3.  The integration pins
target Agent Shell 0.86.1 at ‘137b574’ and Shell Maker 0.97.5 at
‘dcc05a8’.  ACP retains its reviewed snapshot.  A personal package
upgrade does not refresh ‘test/integration-dependencies.json’ or its
prepared tree.  When the preparer rejects a changed lock, choose a fresh
‘TEST_DEPS_DIR’ and pass the same directory to preparation and both
integration gates; preserve the old directory until it is no longer
needed.

   For every pin refresh, compare all Emacsvox navigation,
speech-control, table, and repeat keys against shell and viewport maps,
inherited Shell Maker maps, list editing, inline actions, permission and
question controls, and transients.  Verify effective keys in real prompt
and transcript contexts.  The 0.86.1 refresh keeps the existing scoped
overrides: viewport ‘t’ discovers tables; table ‘r’ reads rows instead
of quoting or replying; viewport table ‘c’ reads columns and ‘a’ selects
table speech instead of continuing or repeating a reply.  The table ‘k’
copy prefix takes precedence over Emacsvox's source-block jump.  These
preserve consistent reading keys; the displaced commands remain
available through ‘M-x’ as documented in the Agent Shell user chapter.
Prompt letters remain literal input, and repeat navigation must release
unrelated keys.

  1. Internal ownership and renderer tests

     The main ‘lisp/emacsvox-agent-shell.el’ module requires the
     internal ‘lisp/emacsvox-agent-shell-render.el’ module.  The
     renderer module depends only on Emacs libraries, with no reverse
     dependency on the main integration.  Its private helper names and
     argument contracts remain available through the main module.

     Owner                  Responsibilities
     --------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Renderer module        Interpret prompt faces and chat label strings,
                            current and legacy status glyphs, semantic
                            fragment provenance, decorative controls, answer
                            body ranges, and legacy clipboard properties.
                            Most helpers inspect supplied strings; fragment
                            classification can also read the current buffer
                            without changing it.
     Main integration       Capture buffer and overlay context; apply user
                            customization, speech limits, and delivery; own
                            response accumulation, event subscriptions,
                            timers, callback generations, navigation,
                            permission handling, activation, and cleanup.

     Renderer metadata supplies presentation and navigation facts; it
     does not authorize permission responses or establish lifecycle
     completion.  Status label customization stays with the main
     integration.  Keep compatibility interpretation together in the
     renderer module, and keep registration and speech side effects in
     the main owner.

     This is the first bounded separation of responsibilities.  Event
     state, navigation and table commands, and permission handling still
     share the main module.  Assess their private dependencies and
     lifecycle contracts before considering another extraction; a
     heading or a line count alone is not a reason to create a module.

     The independent ‘test/emacsvox-agent-shell-render-tests.el’ suite
     runs in the core and integration gates without needing optional
     packages of its own.  It checks property-preserving copies,
     compatibility renderings, group provenance, answer selection, and a
     fresh module load without integration registrations.  With the
     Emacs selected by ‘local.mk’, it can also run directly:

          /path/to/emacs -Q --batch -L lisp \
            -l test/emacsvox-agent-shell-render-tests.el -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit

     Keep the existing Agent Shell integration tests alongside these
     smaller contracts.  They exercise real pinned upstream renderers,
     speech delivery, enable/disable, and buffer-death cleanup.  After
     preparing the pinned dependencies with ‘make test-deps’, run source
     and compiled parity checks:

          make integration-test
          make graphical-integration-test
          make bytecode
          make bytecode-check
          EMACSVOX_AGENT_SHELL_TEST_LOAD=compiled make integration-test
          EMACSVOX_AGENT_SHELL_TEST_LOAD=compiled make graphical-integration-test

     The environment setting selects the exact renderer and main
     integration ‘.elc’ files in a fresh Emacs; other dependencies
     retain the runner's source preference.  Without it, both modules
     load explicitly from ‘.el’ sources.  The tests capture speech
     events and do not establish audible playback.

  2. Upstream contract layers

     The integration uses three deliberately different contract layers:

     Layer         Agent Shell owner           Emacsvox rule
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Public        ‘agent-shell-subscribe-to’, Primary authority for
     structured    ‘agent-shell-unsubscribe’,  processing, completion,
     API           ‘agent-shell-status’, and   errors, permission urgency,
                   ‘agent-shell-shell-buffer’; and tool status.  The public
                   lifecycle, permission,      turn boundary triggers
                   and tool events             inspection of renderer-owned
                                               response sections.  Event and
                                               response data is treated as
                                               untrusted content and bounded
                                               before automatic Aural
                                               submission.
     Experimental  ‘agent-shell-ui-state’,     Feature-detected navigation
     renderer      ‘agent-shell-ui-section’,   and presentation input.
     metadata      rendered Markdown           Renderer group provenance
                   table/source properties,    outranks provider-controlled
                   and renderer-owned faces    IDs; prose and IDs cannot
                                               manufacture structured
                                               permission or lifecycle
                                               authority.
     Private       The aggregate               Kept in narrow adapter
     compatibility ‘agent-shell--state’,       functions for the supported
     fallback      viewport position           minimum or incomplete
                   helpers, configuration      renderers.  A missing optional
                   access on 0.85.3, and       detail may reduce header or
                   face, prompt, or ID         navigation richness but must
                   inference                   not create a permission action
                                               or completed response.

     The lifecycle subscription currently consumes ‘init-started’,
     ‘init-finished’, ‘session-selected’, ‘session-restored’,
     ‘input-submitted’, ‘turn-complete’, ‘error’, ‘elicitation-request’,
     and ‘elicitation-response’.  Dedicated subscriptions consume
     ‘permission-request’, ‘permission-response’, and
     ‘tool-call-update’.  Add new semantic behavior at the public event
     layer when Agent Shell exposes it; keep renderer and private
     fallbacks isolated and covered by compatibility tests.

     Question feedback resolves control identity from renderer
     properties and values from the owning shell's ‘:elicitations’
     state.  Navigation and actions work in both shell and viewport
     buffers.  Public question events govern automatic request and
     response announcements; action advice confirms edits and previews.
     No new key overrides are installed.  The upstream feature stays
     opt-in.

     Configuration access uses ‘agent-shell-config-options’ and
     ‘agent-shell-initialized-p’ when available.  The 0.85.3 fallback
     reads normalized private options and checks the initialization
     pipeline flags.  Startup defaults must not be announced as user
     changes.  Verify the minimum at ‘f44c96b’ as well as the pinned
     version, including source and compiled speech and graphical form
     controls.  Use separate prepared dependency directories for each
     version.

     The lifecycle subscription also observes ‘config-option-update’.
     Configuration snapshots suppress initial inventory and duplicate
     callback/event confirmation.  Recent Agent Shell versions emit this
     event on successful setting requests too; the callback fallback
     remains for older versions and shares the same snapshot cache.
     Confirmations reuse upstream completion labels when available so
     grouped choices with identical names remain distinguishable.  Late
     tool details are compared only for permissions established by a
     public ‘permission-request’ event, and their snapshots are removed
     on resolution.

     Current Agent Shell omits rendered Codex ‘rawOutput’ and textual
     diffs from ‘tool-call-update’.  A narrow
     ‘agent-shell--on-notification’ adapter joins that public event to
     the body rendered during the same call, matching both the owning
     buffer and qualified fragment ID.  Full tool speech is submitted
     after rendering returns.  Output digests and lifecycle statuses are
     tracked separately so late output is spoken without repeating
     identical updates.  Renderer IDs without a matching public event do
     not authorize tool feedback.

     Until upstream exposes public steering and push lifecycle events,
     small adapters wrap the structured steering request's callbacks and
     the accepted ‘session/push’ lifecycle.  Push speech finishes before
     the original completion callback can drain the next queued prompt.
     Callback generations invalidate pending speech feedback on session
     change or integration cleanup; original transport callbacks still
     run.  No transcript text is interpreted as a steering outcome or
     push completion.

     The queue adapter captures the pending count before draining and
     lets ‘input-submitted’ confirm how many prompts actually left the
     queue.  This covers both one-at-a-time and merged submission
     without duplicating upstream policy.  Prompt list editing and diff
     entry have native command feedback.  New asynchronous tool, queue,
     steering, setting, and permission-detail submissions explicitly
     select the notification lane; list editing and diff entry remain
     foreground interactions.  All new events and their outcome, count,
     or setting attributes are registered by the workflow semantic
     provider so Aural rules can customize their presentation.

20.3.15 Magit contracts
-----------------------

Staging and selected synchronous operations collect Git outcomes at
‘magit-process-finish’, before Magit can raise an error.  One
interactive command owns the aggregate result across nested calls.  A
process that Magit starts and waits for contributes only to the
collector attached when it was started; unrelated process callbacks
cannot change that result.  Uncollected asynchronous completions
explicitly select the notification lane.  Neither a command's return
value nor the absence of an Emacs error proves Git succeeded.

   Successful staging and unstaging in a Magit view present the
refreshed line with destination section facts and a ‘focus-entered’
event.  The destination does not inherit the original selection's staged
or unstaged state.  Failures, commands that run no Git operation, and
commands outside Magit views retain outcome messages.  Regression
coverage enables real refreshes for file and hunk staging and unstaging,
including removal of the last file in a section.

   Magit's positional highlight overlays must not become source
personalities.  The integration suppresses legacy overlay voice
mirroring for those highlights and excludes their faces from source
capture.  It preserves text faces, deliberate personalities, and
customized hash voices.  Navigation still emits section facts; empty
lines supply the core line-condition facts without a competing selection
icon.

   Regression tests use isolated repositories for real Git failures and
real section trees for highlighting and visibility.  Run the Magit tests
from source, then again in a fresh Emacs with
‘EMACSVOX_MAGIT_TEST_LOAD=compiled’ after bringing byte-code current.  A
live buffer created with the former highlight mirroring needs a Magit
refresh to rebuild its source properties.

20.3.16 WSL2 Omnivox development staging
----------------------------------------

The ordinary WSL2 user route is implemented by
‘bin/emacsvox-wsl-install’ and pinned by ‘etc/wsl-install.conf’.  Treat
either file as release tooling.  When advancing GNU Emacs or Omnivox,
update the version, archive name, URL, and SHA-256 together; exercise
x64 and ARM64 selection in the isolated installer tests; and perform one
real installation of the newly pinned archive before updating the user
manual.  The installer must remain per-user, must not invoke ‘sudo’ or
edit shell startup files, and must preserve an explicit ‘EMACS’ or
‘local.mk’ selection rather than silently replacing it.

   The installed Omnivox executable is recorded as an absolute WSL path
in the data-only ‘$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program’ file.
Launcher selection is explicit ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’, that per-user file,
developer staging, then ‘PATH’.  Keep the selection tests synchronized
with this order.  A configured but invalid path is a visible
configuration error; silently falling through would hide a stale or
damaged user installation.

   This section describes the reproducible source-build path, not the
ordinary pre-built WSL2 installation.  The complete source-build
prerequisite checklist is in *note Building Omnivox For WSL2: Building
Omnivox For WSL2.  Dependency ownership is deliberately split across
three boundaries:

Boundary      Supplied by            Dependencies
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Windows and   Maintainer             WSL interoperation,
WSL host                             ‘powershell.exe’, ‘wslpath’,
                                     Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or
                                     newer, Docker with Linux/AMD64
                                     support, Git, Make, download and
                                     checksum tools, standard GNU shell
                                     utilities, network access, and
                                     writable Windows ‘LocalAppData’.
Pinned        Emacsvox target        The immutable Rust/Debian
release                              container, MinGW cross tools and
toolchain                            runtime DLLs, CMake, Clang and
                                     libclang, eSpeak build
                                     dependencies, Roslyn, .NET 4.0
                                     reference assemblies, and official
                                     Windows Piper companion.  The exact
                                     versions, source commit, and
                                     downloaded-package hashes live in
                                     ‘servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock’.
Build         Git checkouts          The current Emacsvox checkout and
source                               the Omnivox checkout selected by
                                     ‘OMNIVOX_DIR’, including Omnivox's
                                     locked Rust dependencies and
                                     checksum-locked Flite and RuTTS
                                     source inputs.

   ‘make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain’ prepares the cached Roslyn
inputs, builds the Docker image, and validates this boundary before
compilation.  The full target then builds Omnivox's two 32-bit helper
sources deterministically, builds native Windows Omnivox plus its
RHVoice, Flite, and RuTTS helpers in the container, and stages their
licenses, provenance, and checksums.  The clean target also builds Piper
support and verifies that the pinned companion and main executable
identify the same Omnivox source commit.  A host Rust installation,
MinGW installation, Visual Studio, .NET SDK, or system eSpeak
installation is not an input to this release path.  RHVoice's native
library, languages, and voices remain external user-supplied runtime
inputs.  Flite's CMU US SLT voice is compiled into its checksum-locked
helper.  RuTTS's built-in male and female Russian voices are compiled
from the checksum-locked 6.3.3 source; RuLex is not included.

   After staging, the static verifier rechecks manifests, provenance,
the Windows-local copy, and all configured external inputs.  The
separate live verifier executes that exact Windows-local runtime,
prewarms the bounded eSpeak voice cache tied to the verified
content-addressed data, discovers the compiled Flite and RuTTS voices,
and requires real WAV synthesis from both helpers.  Use ‘make
verify-windows-omnivox-live’ to repeat only the executable smoke gate.

   ‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’ is an optional staging input, not a release
payload.  When supplied, the target validates the adjacent
configuration, copies both files into a content-addressed Windows-local
model cache, records their hashes, and writes the Windows path used by
the launcher.  The recursive runtime manifest deliberately excludes
model files.  A different model therefore creates a distinct local build
identity without changing the pinned companion.  The public-domain
engineering voice used in Omnivox CI is approved only for that bounded
test purpose and is not a model-distribution default.

   Maintainers testing tracked Emacsvox or Omnivox changes may use ‘make
windows-omnivox-dev’.  That target records both worktree-diff hashes and
labels the result as a development build.  It builds the RHVoice, Flite,
and RuTTS companions but omits Piper, because the currently pinned Piper
companion must not be mixed with later Omnivox source.  It also rejects
‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’.  Advance the checked Piper companion and source
commit together before these integrations enter the clean release path.
See ‘servers/omnivox-release/README.org’ for the pinned toolchain,
licensing, provenance, and restaging contract.

   To test Piper before that release-pin advance, commit and push a
clean Omnivox revision and run its ‘Piper Native Validation’ workflow.
Download the ‘omnivox-piper-windows-x64’ artifact, which contains the
companion archive and ‘piper-sha256sums.txt’, then stage it with a
reviewed model:

     OMNIVOX_PIPER_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHIVE=/absolute/path/to/omnivox-1.17.0-piper-windows-x64.zip \
       OMNIVOX_PIPER_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHIVE_SHA256=64-character-sha256 \
       OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx \
       make windows-omnivox-piper-dev

   This is a development-only bridge, not a release-pin bypass.  It
verifies the outer archive hash, rejects unsafe archive members and
symbolic links, checks the companion's internal manifest, and requires
its source provenance to name the exact clean Omnivox ‘HEAD’ and the
Windows MSVC target.  The staged runtime records the artifact hash and
companion tree hash and labels the companion
‘github-actions-native-development-build’ with version ‘development-ci’.
The version-looking archive filename comes from Omnivox's current
package metadata and does not assert that post-tag code was released.
The final live gate loads the separately cached model and requires Piper
to enumerate that voice and synthesize a non-empty WAV.

   The ordinary ‘windows-omnivox-dev’ target remains appropriate for
uncommitted Omnivox work because it rebuilds all included companions
locally and omits Piper.  The CI-artifact target deliberately rejects a
dirty Omnivox checkout: commit and push the change, let the native
workflow build that exact commit, then use its new artifact.  Emacsvox
itself may contain tracked changes; their diff hash remains part of the
local runtime identity.

   After one complete development runtime has been staged, use ‘make
windows-omnivox-main-dev’ for changes confined to the main Rust server
or its main-only audio output.  This guarded path uses the same pinned
toolchain to rebuild only ‘omnivox.exe’, derives a new content-addressed
runtime from the current hash-verified development payload, records the
original complete build and reused manifest in provenance, hard-links
the unchanged payload, and runs a focused live check of the new Windows
main executable.  It refuses reuse when helper, helper-protocol,
dependency, toolchain, or companion inputs changed; in that case rerun
‘make windows-omnivox-dev’.  Run ‘make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime
verify-windows-omnivox-live’ when a complete development audit is
useful.  The clean ‘make windows-omnivox’ release path always rebuilds
and verifies every payload and never uses incremental reuse.

   The development target accepts ‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY’ and
‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA’ together, plus an optional
‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG’ directory.  It resolves WSL or Windows absolute
paths, requires the C API DLL and the language/voice data layout,
records content digests in provenance and machine-local Windows path
pointers beside the selected runtime, and leaves all RHVoice files
external.  Those pointers let the launcher restore the variables for
every fresh Emacsvox process.  The runtime verifier rejects missing or
changed external inputs.  Omitting all three variables leaves RHVoice
unconfigured without affecting Flite, RuTTS, or the other engines.
RuTTS requires no external installation; the staged helper always
provides its built-in male and female Russian voices without RuLex.

   Do not bypass the Omnivox release target manually or clean either
repository to make its release guard pass.  Use ‘make windows-omnivox’
only for the reproducible clean-release path documented in the
user-facing WSL2 route.

20.3.17 Windows staging implementation and fixtures
---------------------------------------------------

Make owns option and clean-source guards, toolchain verification,
companion preparation, Docker compilation, and final runtime
verification.  Its ‘windows-omnivox’ target passes prepared build
outputs to the internal ‘servers/omnivox-release/stage-runtime.sh’
script.  Use the guarded Make targets rather than invoking this script
directly.

   TGSpeechBox is included by default.  Its helper compiles inside the
pinned container with the pinned MinGW POSIX C++ compiler.  The internal
‘build-tgspeechbox.py’ records the executable, data and compiler hashes.
On the host it validates those outputs and uses the exact Windows helper
to generate voice inventories before runtime staging.  A changed
executable, data payload or source lock fails this handoff.  Clean
builds require TGSpeechBox; development builds can explicitly omit it.

   The script owns host-side payload staging: input checks, content
hashes, optional voice-data caches, diagnostics, provenance, path files,
and selection of the completed runtime.  Make passes the Emacsvox root
as ‘EMACSVOX_STAGE_ROOT’, the resolved ‘OMNIVOX_*’ source/build/runtime
paths and feature switches, and the two pinned Roslyn/reference-assembly
archive hashes.  The assignments at the call site and the script's
initial parameter checks define the complete internal input list.
Optional user inputs retain their ‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’,
‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_*’, and ‘OMNIVOX_ECI_DLL’ environment variables.
Main-only development staging remains separately owned by
‘stage-main-dev.sh’ with its existing reuse restrictions.

   Run ‘make windows-staging-test’ on a GNU/Linux host with Python 3,
Git, Make, and the standard GNU file/checksum tools.  The tests use
private repositories, prepared payloads, runtime directories, and a
simulated LocalAppData.  Docker, Windows commands, and compiler/verifier
boundaries are replaced by fixtures; Git guards, copying, hashing,
provenance, and link activation execute normally.  The suite is also
included in ‘make test’ and its own CI job.  It exercises
clean/development variants, optional companions and external voice
inputs, paths containing spaces, corrupt caches, missing inputs,
rejected main-only reuse, interruption, and activation failure.

   Preserve the current activation sequence during maintenance.  The
script selects the staged runtime before Make's final runtime and live
checks; a failure in those final checks does not restore the previous
selection.  Interruption before activation can leave temporary files in
a new version directory while preserving the current link.  The fixture
tests characterize these existing behaviors and successful retry.
Changing cleanup or rollback requires a separate fix and acceptance
decision.  Fixture success does not establish native compiler
reproducibility, real Windows path conversion, engine startup, or
audible playback; run the existing native gates for that acceptance in
an appropriate staging environment.

20.3.18 Aural experiments and guided changes
--------------------------------------------

Physical experiments use the common voice editor with an exact
engine/voice selector, an immutable opening snapshot, a mutable working
style and undo history.  Navigation uses the ordinary voice.
Experiments write no settings; Keep prepares a named-voice draft,
including any reviewed row placement and adjustment scope.  The
destination still requires an explicit save.

   The unified palette contract is recorded in ADR 0018.  Every nonroot
palette uses complete schema 3 entries and an explicit parent chain to
‘acss-default’.  Palette definitions and immutable local-choice
snapshots are saved through the common coordinator.  Frozen proposals
retain separate persistence and apply completion, and retry does not
repeat a successful write.  Save failures, acknowledged application and
actual playback evidence remain separate.  Workstation routing profiles
contain global engine policy, not per-name routes.

   Labelled comparisons use one controlled preview sequence, with
identical sample text and separately configurable labels.  Each sequence
belongs to its speech lane.  Stopping that lane cancels remaining
entries and invalidates late callbacks; stopping foreground speech must
not cancel notifications.  Requested parameters and advertised
capabilities are distinct from playback reports.  Do not fabricate exact
effective numeric values when the adapter reports only support,
acceptance, or degradation.

   Guided feedback changes use existing declarative rules and registered
facts.  The draft selects a component, matching criteria, and lifetime
separately; preview dynamically overlays the intended layer without
mutating live rules.  Explicit buffer-rule snapshots in captured context
must also be overlaid.  Generated IDs remain stable for the matched
component; new rules follow existing explicit order within the layer.
Apply checks a Current-item source guard and refuses to overwrite
another unfinished editor for the same scope.  Historical selection
chooses a frozen part when a transaction contains several runs.
Original replay uses that part; proposed preview deliberately resolves
it with current rules and the captured buffer context.

   Shared Help and offline task nodes preserve the original source and
exact return position.  New interfaces should use the shared parent
modes and add a task-node mapping to ‘emacsvox-aural-ui-manual-nodes’.
Row action predicates should hide operations that cannot apply to that
row; this includes editing built-in options and reordering items outside
the active-order view.

20.3.19 Repeatable voice performance measurements
-------------------------------------------------

Use ‘utils/voice_benchmark.py’ to retain comparable measurements of
ordinary speech, native voice settings and the voice editor.  It starts
private speech workers and fresh Emacs processes; it does not connect to
a running Emacs or save editor drafts.  The default null audio output
avoids speaking the workload.  Reports remain below the ignored
‘.benchmarks/’ directory.

   The runner requires Python 3, the selected Emacs toolchain, and an
Omnivox checkout containing ‘tools/benchmark_server.py’ and
‘tools/process_metrics.py’.  It reuses that checkout's lifecycle
workloads.  On Linux, ‘xvfb-run’ provides a private graphical display
when installed.  The plan can explicitly select ‘native’ for a visible
graphical Emacs or ‘batch’ for a nongraphical run.  Batch measurements
do not establish graphical responsiveness.  Build current byte-code
before running; the runner refuses stale byte-code.

   Create a local plan once, using the same runtime overrides as the
development speech launcher.  List the runtime libraries, dictionaries
and voice data that affect the selected engines.  For example:

     python3 utils/voice_benchmark.py init .benchmarks/voices.json \
       --program /path/to/omnivox \
       --emacs /path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs \
       --omnivox-root /path/to/omnivox-checkout \
       --runtime-input voices=/path/to/voice-data

   Initialization captures supported runtime overrides from the
environment, including ‘OMNIVOX_DECTALK_DLL’, ‘OMNIVOX_ECI_DLL’ and
‘ESPEAK_NG_DATA’.  Inspect the resulting JSON plan.  Its initial routes
are Eloquence ‘v1’, DECtalk ‘paul’ and eSpeak's British English voice
(‘espeak:gmw/en’, with a backslash on Windows); remove unavailable
engines or change exact voice IDs.  Native routes also specify one
parameter and test value.  A missing voice, fallback voice, unsupported
parameter or failed native readback fails the workload.  It must not
become a fast but invalid result.

   If a separately retained failure prevents one case from producing
timings, a route may declare ‘exclude_cases’ as an object mapping case
names to reasons.  Exclusions appear at the top of the report and must
match on both sides of a comparison.  An excluded case supplies no
evidence about its performance or correctness; do not treat a completed
timing run as clearing that failure.

   The plan pins the executable path.  When staging a new development
build, update ‘targets[].program’ before measuring it.  Retain the
earlier executable and complete helper payload if an old-build
comparison is wanted.  ‘--baseline-program’ at initialization adds a
second target for ordinary speech, with native controls and editor tests
disabled for that older worker.  An editor comparison against older Lisp
needs a target with its own ‘emacsvox_root’ and current byte-code for
that checkout.  Keep runtime inputs, rate, display mode, machine and
benchmark driver identical between runs.

   Run a short setup check, then collect the repeatable baseline:

     make voice-benchmark BENCHMARK_PRESET=quick
     make voice-benchmark BENCHMARK_OUTPUT=.benchmarks/before

   The full preset runs three shuffled blocks, discards warm-up samples,
and collects 90 observations per warm speech case and 60 per repeated
editor operation.  Cold speech and first editor openings have fewer
observations and are reported separately.  Allow several minutes for a
full run; engine startup and timeout costs vary.  Keep unrelated
workload low.  Output directories must be new; existing evidence is
never overwritten.  An interrupted or failed run retains its partial
report and logs but cannot be compared as successful.

   The speech cases cover a character, word, line, dense text, multipart
delivery, rapid replacement, and simultaneous main and notification
workers.  For engines with native controls, the runner distinguishes
ordinary timeline delivery, layered delivery with no native overrides,
and an explicit native override.  The editor workload measures opening,
navigation, control readiness, common preview completion and automatic
previews after native adjustments.  It keeps synchronous command time
separate from waiting for asynchronous replies.

   Compare a subsequent full run against the baseline:

     make voice-benchmark BENCHMARK_OUTPUT=.benchmarks/after
     python3 utils/voice_benchmark.py compare \
       .benchmarks/before .benchmarks/after --output .benchmarks/comparison.md

   Use ‘--flavour legacy --mode warm’ to examine ordinary speech, or
‘--flavour editor --mode ui’ for the editor.  A plan containing both
builds can be compared against itself with ‘--before-target baseline
--after-target current’.  Every report contains medians, 95th
percentiles, maxima and raw observations.  The default comparison flags
a series only when its 95th percentile rises by more than both 5
milliseconds and 15 percent, with at least 20 observations on each side.
Short setup checks and low-count cold openings are explicitly
inconclusive.  Repeat flagged runs before attributing a change to code.
With ‘--fail-on-regression’, exit status is 1 for a flagged slowdown, 3
for insufficient evidence, and 2 for an invalid run or comparison.

   The index records executable and helper hashes, runtime input hashes,
driver hashes, source commit and dirty-tree identity, toolchain and host
information.  Comparison validates the retained raw-report hashes and
recomputes summaries.  Changed runtime data or incompatible test
settings are rejected.  Retain the whole run directory, not just
‘report.md’.  The source identity may differ between runs: that is the
change under investigation.

   Add ‘BENCHMARK_RESOURCES=1’ for a separate process-tree CPU and
memory phase.  Sampling runs after the latency jobs so process
enumeration does not distort those timings.  Resource reports identify
their observation provider and its limitations; unavailable memory
evidence is not zero memory use.  Editor reports also retain Emacs CPU,
resident memory and garbage-collection counters.  Check retained
processes and memory across repeated work before calling an increase a
leak.

   These measurements observe software source consumption and
completion, not sound reaching the listener.  Editor previews currently
expose completion but no onset event.  Null audio bypasses the physical
device, so device latency or audible clipping needs a separate listening
or loopback measurement.  Results from null and real audio are not
comparable.  Run ‘make voice-benchmark-test’ to check evidence integrity
and comparison logic.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Authoring Sound Packs,  Up: Integration Maintenance

20.3.20 Authoring Sound Packs
-----------------------------

Ordinary selection, preview, and cue remapping are covered in *note
Sounds, Tones, and Spatial Cues: (emacsvox)Sounds And Space.  Preparing
a new pack requires audio files and resource metadata.  A pack supplies
recordings for registered cue names; the presentation rules decide when
those cues occur.

   The standard personal discovery root is ‘~/.emacsvox/sounds/packs/’,
controlled by ‘emacsvox-aural-personal-sound-packs-directory’.  Each
immediate child is a candidate pack, and its directory basename becomes
its identifier.  Choose an unused identifier.  A directory containing
‘button.ogg’ is discovered without a manifest.  A partial pack can
instead supply the data-only manifest ‘emacsvox-sound-pack.el’ and
inherit missing cues from another pack.

   For example, create ‘my-cues/’ below the personal root and put this
single Lisp data form in ‘my-cues/emacsvox-sound-pack.el’:

     (:schema-version 1
      :summary "My personal cues, inheriting the remaining chimes"
      :parent chimes
      :default-spatialization neutral)

   The file contains data, not executable initialization: do not wrap
the form in ‘setq’, quote it, or add a second form.  Put a replacement
recording such as ‘button.ogg’ in the same directory.  Its basename
names the registered cue.  Unknown cue names fail the resource audit;
introducing a new semantic cue requires an integration to register its
intent and use it.  Every standalone sound pack must resolve ‘button’,
directly or through its declared parent.

   The optional ‘:profiles’ field lists registered cue-coverage
requirements.  These are resource validation contracts, distinct from
Presentation Profiles that select a user's setup.  Keep the coverage
appropriate to the intended uses rather than treating a successful
single audition as complete coverage.  Default spatialization is
‘neutral’, ‘stereo’, or ‘pre-spatialized’.  Use the last for recordings
that already encode their intended spatial placement, so runtime balance
does not position them a second time.

   Open _Sound packs_ with ‘C-e H o p’ and press ‘g’ to rescan.  Inspect
the new pack with ‘<RET>’: the replacement should be native, while
unchanged cues identify their parent or fallback provider.  Audition
individual cues with ‘P’ and validate with ‘v’ before activating the
pack.  Missing or malformed assets are errors to resolve, not cues to
copy from an unrelated installed pack.  No activation follows from
writing the directory or previewing it.

   The manager's ‘e’ edits metadata for discovered packs only.  It
replaces the manifest atomically, retains the previous file with a ‘~’
suffix, and restores the old metadata if validation of the refreshed
pack fails.  It does not edit recordings.  After changing files
externally, refresh the pack; ‘emacsvox-aural-refresh-resource-pack’
refreshes an already registered pack, and
‘emacsvox-aural-refresh-discovered-resource-packs’ rescans discovery
roots.  Engine installation, remote sound-directory placement, and user
startup selection remain separate tasks.


